            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance news is that NASA reported the rover has found evidence that a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rock on the rim of Jezero Crater was likely built by repeated asteroid impacts. The rock sequence, nicknamed the “Broom Point member,” may be more than 3.9 billion years old, making it among the oldest terrain ever examined by a Mars rover【6:3†source】.\n\nWhy it matters: because Mars lacks Earth-style plate tectonics that recycle crust, these rocks may preserve a rare record of the early solar system’s violent impact history—an era largely erased from Earth’s surface【6:3†source】. The rover’s instruments found distinct rock types, including breccias and abundant glassy beads, which NASA says point more strongly to asteroid impacts than volcanism【6:3†source】.\n\nAnother recent milestone: Perseverance has completed a “marathon” on Mars, reaching 26.2 miles / 42.195 km of total driving on its 1,890th Martian day, or sol. It achieved that distance in about five years and four months—much faster than Opportunity, which took more than 11 years to cover the same distance【6:0†source】. NASA also highlighted an orbital HiRISE image showing Perseverance and its tracks near the area west of Jezero Crater【6:0†source】.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-perseverance-rover-reads-record-of-ancient-mars-impacts/",
